    37	         combo    -> start becomes blue, dest becomes blue
    38	       stuck branch with a 1-pixel gate:
    39	         original -> black moves forward to the last 2x2 placement before the gate
    40	         combo    -> black stays at start, red stays at end, output equals input
    41	   - The fusion is not two unrelated edits: the two branches are now maximally
    42	     separated visually. Reachable examples erase both salient blocks; stuck examples
    43	     preserve both blocks exactly.
    44	
    45	b) Complexity calibration
    46	   - Reachable and stuck outputs are easy to distinguish by eye:
    47	       reachable -> no black/red blocks remain
    48	       stuck     -> the scene is unchanged
    49	   - This is strong enough to be learnable from 2-4 examples without adding extra
    50	     markers or hidden bookkeeping.
    51	   - To avoid degeneracy, stuck samples are rejected unless the original task would have
    52	     moved the black square forward by at least one placement. That guarantees the combo
    53	     differs materially from the base task on blocked cases.
    54	
    55	c) Implicit assumption audit for main()
    56	   - Component scan still assumes the two salient non-background objects are solid
    57	     monochromatic squares. Generator preserves that.
    58	   - Picking mover/destination by color order remains safe only because generation uses
    59	     the fixed palette black=0, red=2 and introduces no extra same-sized solid squares.
    60	   - River inference as the most common non-background color still holds because blue is
    61	     the only large non-square structure.
    62	   - Feasibility logic still relies on the full n×n window being traversable in
    63	     {river,mover,destination}. The fusion does not change motion geometry.
    64	   - The original unreachable branch depended on scanning for the farthest reachable
    65	     distance. D13/2 invalidates that assumption, so main() must not choose a fallback
    66	     target there.
    67	   - The original reachable renderer always painted the destination with mover color.
